+++ b/backend/internal/CLAUDE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,78 @@
+# Backend internals
+
+Loaded when you work under `backend/internal/`. It adds to `backend/CLAUDE.md`; it does not repeat it.
+
+## Check your work
+
+Run these from the repo root. Each prints a few lines on success and only the findings on failure.
+
+| When | Command | Cost (warm) |
+|---|---|---|
+| A hook runs it after every edit of a `.go` file | `bash backend/scripts/check.sh fast <file>` | under 1s |
+| After each logical step | `bash backend/scripts/check.sh step` | about 10s |
+| Before you push | `bash backend/scripts/check.sh full` | about 15s |
+
+- `fast` is gofmt plus `go vet` on the edited file's package. Errors in other files of that package are normal in the middle of a change; finish the change, then run `step`.
+- `step` and `full` clear `DATABASE_URL` on purpose (it is the shared Neon database). Every DB-backed test then skips, and the summary says how many. A green run does not cover those; say so in your report.
+- `step` and `full` also run the architecture rules (see Layers). A failure there names the file and the rule; fix the code, never the baseline.
+- Do not report "tests pass" without running `step` or `full` and quoting its summary line.
+
+## The loop
+
+Work in small steps and let the check say when a step is done.
+
+1. Make one logical change.
+2. Run `bash backend/scripts/check.sh step` and read all of its output, not only the last line.
+3. Red: fix the code the failure names. An `arch-check` failure gives the file, the rule and the import or call; remove that import or call (add a port in `core/ports`, inject a clock) instead of working around it. If the same check fails twice for the same reason, re-read the rule before trying another route.
+4. Green: take the next step. After you fix a violation that was in `backend/arch-baseline.txt`, run `bash backend/scripts/arch-check.sh shrink` so the baseline gets smaller.
+5. Before you report, run `full` and put both its summary line and the `arch-check:` line in the report.
+
+If the ticket cannot be done without a new import edge, an exemption or a new rule, stop at that step and say so in the report. Do not edit `backend/arch-baseline.txt`, `backend/.go-arch-lint.yml` or `backend/tools/archcheck` to get past it.
+
+## Names are part of the contract
+
+Tests written against a different implementation fail on names, not on logic. So:
+
+- Use every name the ticket gives, exactly: functions, methods, struct fields, option funcs, error sentinels, SQL fragments it quotes.
+- Where the ticket gives none, copy the shape of the nearest neighbour (the sibling method, the sibling repository, the sibling test) rather than inventing a style.
+- End your report with the list of every new exported name and every new or changed port method.
+- Where the ticket leaves a behaviour open (what "running" or "failed" means, what happens to rows on an error), pick the reading closest to the ticket's own words, say which one you picked at the top of your report, and cover it with a test.
+
+## Layers
+
+Imports point inward: `core/domain` ← `core/ports` ← `core/services` ← `adapters/*`. Wiring (building concrete adapters) happens only in `cmd/*` and `internal/server`. `pkg/*` never imports `internal/*`.
+
+- `core/domain` and `core/ports` import the standard library and each other, nothing else.
+- Services import ports and domain, never an adapter, `gorm`, `pgx`, `gqlgen`, `chi` or `net/http`.
+- Adapters do not import each other and do not import `internal/demo`.
+
+`bash backend/scripts/arch-check.sh` enforces this, and `check.sh step` and `full` run it:
+
+- **B1 core isolation, B3 adapter isolation** (go-arch-lint, rules in `backend/.go-arch-lint.yml`): each component may import only what its entry lists. A new edge fails until someone decides it belongs.
+- **B2 mutation auth chain:** every Mutation and Subscription field in the schema has `@auth` or `@owner`, has a resolver, and that resolver derives the actor itself (`auth.RequireAuth`, `auth.ForContext` or `auth.UserRowForContext`, directly or through a helper in the same package).
+- **B7 injected clock:** no `time.Now()` call in `core/services` or the repositories, except inside a function named `clock` or `now`.
+
+What already breaks is listed in `backend/arch-baseline.txt` (23 lines: services importing adapters, resolvers importing `adapters/realtime` and `adapters/tmdb`, adapters importing `internal/demo`, `setPassageDisplayTitle` with no actor, 11 `time.Now()` calls). Only a violation that is not on that list fails. They are not precedent: do not copy them.
+
+**Never edit `backend/arch-baseline.txt` or `backend/.go-arch-lint.yml` to make a check pass.** The baseline may shrink, never grow: after you fix a baselined violation, run `bash backend/scripts/arch-check.sh shrink`. The check also fails if the baseline has a line it did not start with. A new rule, an exemption or a changed import allowance needs the owner's approval: stop and ask.
+
+If the work needs a dependency that points the wrong way, add a port in `core/ports` and an adapter that implements it.
+
+## Conventions the code follows
+
+- **Repositories:** file `gorm_<entity>_repository.go`, type `Gorm<Entity>Repository`, with a compile-time `var _ port.X = (*Impl)(nil)`.
+- **Services:** the port and its implementation share a name; import the port package as `portservices`.
+- **Actor parameter:** `actorUserID` is the usual name for the acting user on a service method (13 of the 24 mutators checked; the rest use `actor`, `userID` or `viewerID`). Use it.
+- **Mutation resolvers:** `auth.RequireAuth(ctx)` first, the `@auth` directive in the schema, and never a user id from the client (see Gotchas in `backend/CLAUDE.md`).
+- **Errors:** return the sentinels in `core/domain/errors.go`, wrapped with `%w`; test with `errors.Is`. Never branch on an error's text. Resolvers log, then flatten to `fmt.Errorf("...: %v")` at the API boundary; nowhere else.
+- **Time:** the cached repositories keep a `now func() time.Time` field (default `time.Now`) that tests replace (`adapters/repositories/cached/user_repository.go`). Services and the postgres repositories still call `time.Now()` directly in 11 places (baselined); arch-check fails on a twelfth. Give new code a clock the same way: a `now func() time.Time` field defaulting to `time.Now` (a reference, not a call, so it passes), or a helper named `clock` or `now`.
+- **Mocks:** hand-written `...Mock`/`...Fake`/`...Stub` types in `_test.go` files, declared per test package. Adding a method to a port breaks every implementer, so `grep` the `_test.go` files for them before you add one.
+- **Tests live in two places:** beside the code (in-package) and in `backend/test/*` (external). Put a new test next to its neighbours.
+
+## Docs that are out of date
+
+Do not follow these over the code: `.docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` (names sentinels that do not exist: `ErrUnauthorized`, `ErrConflict`, `ErrValidation`), the claim in `backend/CLAUDE.md` that user and category repositories hand-roll `encodeCursor`/`decodeCursor` (they do not), and its list of five per-domain resolver files (there are seven: add `import` and `user_todo`).
+
+## Writing a ticket for this code
+
+`docs/ticket-template-backend.md`.
diff --git a/backend/scripts/arch-check.sh b/backend/scripts/arch-check.sh
new file mode 100755
index 00000000..ffe782f8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/backend/scripts/arch-check.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,173 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+# Architecture rules B1, B2, B3 and B7 for the backend, with a baseline of the violations that already exist.
+# Run from anywhere. Works with macOS's bash 3.2 and BSD tools.
+#
+#   arch-check.sh [check]   run both checkers. Fails on a violation that is not in the baseline, on a baseline
+#                           entry that no longer occurs, and on a baseline that has grown since it was created.
+#   arch-check.sh shrink    remove the baseline entries that no longer occur. It never adds one.
+#   arch-check.sh init      write the first baseline from the current findings. Refuses if one exists.
+#
+# Rules and tools:
+#   B1 core isolation, B3 adapter isolation    go-arch-lint, configured in .go-arch-lint.yml
+#   B2 mutation auth chain, B7 injected clock  tools/archcheck (a small go/types checker, its own Go module)
+#
+# The baseline (arch-baseline.txt) lists the violations that existed when the rules were introduced, one line
+# each: rule, file, detail, tab-separated, no line numbers. It may shrink and never grow. "Never grow" is
+# checked against the commit that added the file, or against ARCH_BASELINE_REF when that is set.
+#
+# DATABASE_URL is cleared: nothing here may reach the shared database.
+
+set -u
+BACKEND=$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/.." && pwd)
+cd "$BACKEND" || exit 2
+unset DATABASE_URL
+
+BASELINE=arch-baseline.txt
+MAX_LINES=${CHECK_MAX_LINES:-40}
+LINT_VERSION=v1.19.2
+HEADER="# Architecture violations that existed when rules B1, B2, B3 and B7 were introduced.
+# One line per violation: rule, file, detail (tab-separated; line numbers are not recorded).
+# This file may shrink, never grow. Do not add a line to make a check pass: fix the code.
+# Remove entries that were fixed with: bash backend/scripts/arch-check.sh shrink"
+
+TMP=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/arch-check.XXXXXX")
+trap 'rm -rf "$TMP"' EXIT
+
+command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "arch-check: jq is required" >&2; exit 2; }
+
+cap() {
+  awk -v max="$MAX_LINES" 'NR <= max { print } END { if (NR > max) printf "  ... %d more lines\n", NR - max }'
+}
+
+# collect: writes $TMP/current.tsv (rule, file, detail, line), sorted, and $TMP/cur.keys (rule, file, detail).
+collect() {
+  local toolchain
+  toolchain=$(go env GOVERSION)
+
+  # go-arch-lint exits 1 when it finds something, so judge it by whether it produced a report.
+  GOTOOLCHAIN=$toolchain go run "github.com/fe3dback/go-arch-lint@$LINT_VERSION" check --project-path . --json >"$TMP/lint.json" 2>"$TMP/lint.err"
+  if ! jq -e '.Payload' "$TMP/lint.json" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
+    echo "arch-check: go-arch-lint did not produce a report:" >&2
+    head -n 20 "$TMP/lint.err" >&2
+    exit 2
+  fi
+  jq -r '
+    def rel: sub("^/"; "");
+    def imp: sub("^github.com/CodeWarrior-debug/perspectize/backend/"; "");
+    ((.Payload.ArchWarningsDeps // [])[] |
+        [ (if (.ComponentName | IN("domain", "ports", "services")) then "B1" else "B3" end),
+          (.FileRelativePath | rel),
+          (.ComponentName + " must not import " + (.ResolvedImportName | imp)),
+          (.Reference.Line | tostring) ] | @tsv),
+    ((.Payload.ArchWarningsNotMatched // [])[] |
+        [ "B3", (.FileRelativePath | rel), "file belongs to no component in .go-arch-lint.yml", "0" ] | @tsv)
+  ' "$TMP/lint.json" >"$TMP/lint.tsv"
+
+  if ! go -C tools/archcheck run . -root "$BACKEND" >"$TMP/archcheck.out" 2>"$TMP/archcheck.err"; then
+    echo "arch-check: archcheck failed:" >&2
+    head -n 20 "$TMP/archcheck.err" >&2
+    exit 2
+  fi
+  # archcheck prints rule, file, line, detail; the baseline key drops the line.
+  awk -F'\t' '{ print $1 "\t" $2 "\t" $4 "\t" $3 }' "$TMP/archcheck.out" >"$TMP/archcheck.tsv"
+
+  cat "$TMP/lint.tsv" "$TMP/archcheck.tsv" | sort >"$TMP/current.tsv"
+  cut -f1-3 "$TMP/current.tsv" | sort >"$TMP/cur.keys"
+}
+
+# entries <file>: the baseline's data lines, sorted.
+entries() { grep -v -e '^#' -e '^$' "$1" | sort; }
+
+show() { # show <keys file>: print each finding's file:line and detail, for the keys in the file
+  local k line
+  while IFS= read -r k; do
+    awk -F'\t' -v key="$k" '($1 "\t" $2 "\t" $3) == key { printf "  %s:%s  %s  %s\n", $2, $4, $1, $3 }' "$TMP/current.tsv"
+  done <"$1"
+}
+
+# growth: fail if the working baseline has entries that the original baseline did not.
+growth() {
+  local ref=${ARCH_BASELINE_REF:-}
+  if [ -z "$ref" ]; then
+    ref=$(git log --diff-filter=A --format=%H -- "$BASELINE" 2>/dev/null | tail -n 1)
+  fi
+  if [ -z "$ref" ] || ! git show "$ref:./$BASELINE" >"$TMP/ref.baseline" 2>/dev/null; then
+    NOTE="the baseline is not committed yet, so growth was not checked."
+    return 0
+  fi
+  entries "$TMP/ref.baseline" >"$TMP/ref.keys"
+  comm -13 "$TMP/ref.keys" "$TMP/base.keys" >"$TMP/grown.keys"
+  [ ! -s "$TMP/grown.keys" ]
+}
+
+cmd_check() {
+  local failed=0 new stale
+  NOTE=""
+  if [ ! -f "$BASELINE" ]; then
+    echo "arch-check: $BASELINE is missing. It is committed with the rules; restore it with git." >&2
+    exit 2
+  fi
+  collect
+  entries "$BASELINE" >"$TMP/base.keys"
+  comm -13 "$TMP/base.keys" "$TMP/cur.keys" >"$TMP/new.keys"
+  comm -23 "$TMP/base.keys" "$TMP/cur.keys" >"$TMP/stale.keys"
+  new=$(wc -l <"$TMP/new.keys" | tr -d ' ')
+  stale=$(wc -l <"$TMP/stale.keys" | tr -d ' ')
+
+  if [ "$new" -gt 0 ]; then
+    failed=1
+    printf 'arch-check: FAILED. %s new architecture violation(s). Fix the code; do not add them to the baseline:\n' "$new"
+    show "$TMP/new.keys" | cap
+    echo
+  fi
+  if [ "$stale" -gt 0 ]; then
+    failed=1
+    printf 'arch-check: FAILED. %s baseline entr(ies) no longer occur (fixed). Remove them with: bash backend/scripts/arch-check.sh shrink\n' "$stale"
+    cut -f1-3 "$TMP/stale.keys" | tr '\t' ' ' | sed 's/^/  /' | cap
+    echo
+  fi
+  if ! growth; then
+    failed=1
+    printf 'arch-check: FAILED. %s baseline entr(ies) are not in the original baseline. The baseline may shrink, never grow:\n' "$(wc -l <"$TMP/grown.keys" | tr -d ' ')"
+    tr '\t' ' ' <"$TMP/grown.keys" | sed 's/^/  /' | cap
+    echo
+  fi
+  if [ "$failed" -eq 0 ]; then
+    printf 'arch-check: OK (0 new; %s known violations in the baseline)\n' "$(wc -l <"$TMP/base.keys" | tr -d ' ')"
+    [ -z "$NOTE" ] || printf '  %s\n' "$NOTE"
+    return 0
+  fi
+  return 1
+}
+
+cmd_shrink() {
+  [ -f "$BASELINE" ] || { echo "arch-check: $BASELINE is missing" >&2; exit 2; }
+  collect
+  entries "$BASELINE" >"$TMP/base.keys"
+  {
+    printf '%s\n' "$HEADER"
+    comm -12 "$TMP/base.keys" "$TMP/cur.keys"
+  } >"$TMP/shrunk"
+  cp "$TMP/shrunk" "$BASELINE"
+  printf 'arch-check: baseline now has %s entries\n' "$(entries "$BASELINE" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')"
+}
+
+cmd_init() {
+  if [ -f "$BASELINE" ]; then
+    echo "arch-check: $BASELINE already exists. init only writes the first baseline; use shrink to remove entries." >&2
+    exit 2
+  fi
+  collect
+  {
+    printf '%s\n' "$HEADER"
+    cat "$TMP/cur.keys"
+  } >"$BASELINE"
+  printf 'arch-check: wrote %s with %s entries\n' "$BASELINE" "$(wc -l <"$TMP/cur.keys" | tr -d ' ')"
+}
+
+case "${1:-check}" in
+  check) cmd_check ;;
+  shrink) cmd_shrink ;;
+  init) cmd_init ;;
+  *) echo "usage: arch-check.sh [check | shrink | init]" >&2; exit 2 ;;
+esac
diff --git a/backend/scripts/check.sh b/backend/scripts/check.sh
new file mode 100755
index 00000000..b11ab6a8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/backend/scripts/check.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,148 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+# Backend checks in three tiers. Run from anywhere. Works with macOS's bash 3.2 and BSD tools.
+#
+#   check.sh fast <file>...  after editing a file (the PostToolUse hook runs this): gofmt, then go vet on
+#                            the packages the files belong to. ~0.3s with a warm build cache; the first
+#                            run in a fresh checkout also builds the dependencies (~15s).
+#   check.sh step            after each logical step: gofmt, go build ./..., go vet ./..., the architecture
+#                            rules (arch-check.sh), go test ./... with the test cache on. ~10s warm.
+#   check.sh full            before you push: step with the test cache off, plus the perf-tagged vet.
+#                            ~15s warm.
+#
+# Prints a few lines on success. On failure prints the findings (capped at CHECK_MAX_LINES lines per
+# section) and exits 1.
+#
+# DATABASE_URL is cleared on purpose. It is the shared Neon database and no check may touch it. A green
+# run here therefore skips every DB-backed test; the step and full summaries say how many.
+
+set -u
+BACKEND=$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/.." && pwd)
+cd "$BACKEND" || exit 2
+unset DATABASE_URL
+
+MAX_LINES=${CHECK_MAX_LINES:-40}
+now() { perl -MTime::HiRes=time -e 'printf "%.3f", time'; }
+START=$(now)
+REPORT=()
+TESTLINE=""
+ARCHLINE=""
+NOTE=""
+
+# cap: print the first MAX_LINES lines of stdin and say how many were left out.
+cap() {
+  awk -v max="$MAX_LINES" 'NR <= max { print } END { if (NR > max) printf "... %d more lines; run the command yourself for all of it\n", NR - max }'
+}
+
+# add_section <title> <body>: record one failing section.
+add_section() { REPORT+=("$1"$'\n'"$(printf '%s\n' "$2" | cap)"); }
+
+is_generated() { head -n 5 "$1" 2>/dev/null | grep -q 'Code generated .* DO NOT EDIT'; }
+
+finish() {
+  local tier=$1 elapsed
+  elapsed=$(printf '%.1f' "$(echo "$(now) - $START" | bc)")
+  if [ ${#REPORT[@]} -eq 0 ]; then
+    printf 'check %s: OK (%ss)\n' "$tier" "$elapsed"
+    [ -n "$ARCHLINE" ] && printf '  %s\n' "$ARCHLINE"
+    [ -n "$TESTLINE" ] && printf '  %s\n' "$TESTLINE"
+    [ -n "$NOTE" ] && printf '  %s\n' "$NOTE"
+    return 0
+  fi
+  printf 'check %s: FAILED (%ss)\n\n' "$tier" "$elapsed"
+  printf '%s\n\n' "${REPORT[@]}"
+  [ -n "$NOTE" ] && printf '%s\n' "$NOTE"
+  return 1
+}
+
+# format_check <path>...: gofmt -l, ignoring generated files.
+format_check() {
+  local bad=() f
+  while IFS= read -r f; do
+    [ -n "$f" ] || continue
+    is_generated "$f" || bad+=("$f")
+  done < <(gofmt -l "$@" 2>&1)
+  [ ${#bad[@]} -eq 0 ] || add_section "gofmt: not formatted (fix: gofmt -w ${bad[*]})" "$(printf '%s\n' "${bad[@]}")"
+}
+
+# arch_check: the architecture rules, with the baseline of violations that already exist.
+arch_check() {
+  local out
+  if out=$(bash "$BACKEND/scripts/arch-check.sh" 2>&1); then
+    ARCHLINE=$(printf '%s\n' "$out" | sed -n '1p')
+  else
+    add_section "arch-check (architecture rules B1, B2, B3, B7)" "$out"
+  fi
+}
+
+# run_tests <go test flag>...: go test ./... summarised by gotest-summary.py.
+run_tests() {
+  local json summary rc
+  json=$(mktemp)
+  go test ${1+"$@"} -json ./... >"$json" 2>&1
+  summary=$(python3 "$BACKEND/scripts/gotest-summary.py" <"$json")
+  rc=$?
+  rm -f "$json"
+  TESTLINE=$(printf '%s\n' "$summary" | sed -n '1p')
+  NOTE=$(printf '%s\n' "$summary" | sed -n 's/^NOTE: //p')
+  if [ $rc -ne 0 ]; then
+    add_section "go test ./... ($TESTLINE)" "$(printf '%s\n' "$summary" | sed '1d;/^NOTE: /d')"
+  fi
+}
+
+tier_fast() {
+  local files=() pkgs=() uniq=() f rel p vet others
+  for f in "$@"; do
+    case "$f" in "$BACKEND"/*) rel=${f#"$BACKEND"/} ;; /*) continue ;; *) rel=$f ;; esac
+    case "$rel" in *.go) ;; *) continue ;; esac
+    [ -f "$rel" ] || continue
+    is_generated "$rel" && continue
+    files+=("$rel")
+    pkgs+=("./$(dirname "$rel")")
+  done
+  [ ${#files[@]} -gt 0 ] || return 0
+  while IFS= read -r p; do uniq+=("$p"); done < <(printf '%s\n' "${pkgs[@]}" | sort -u)
+
+  format_check "${files[@]}"
+  if ! vet=$(go vet "${uniq[@]}" 2>&1); then
+    vet=$(printf '%s\n' "$vet" | grep -v '^# ')
+    add_section "go vet ${uniq[*]} (compile errors and vet findings)" "$vet"
+    # Mid-change, an edit can break files you have not got to yet; say so only when that is what happened.
+    others=$(printf '%s\n' "$vet" | grep -oE '[A-Za-z0-9_./-]+\.go:[0-9]+' | sed 's/:[0-9]*$//' | sort -u | grep -vxF -f <(printf '%s\n' "${files[@]}"))
+    [ -z "$others" ] || NOTE="Some errors are in files you did not edit just now ($(printf '%s' "$others" | tr '\n' ' ')). That is normal mid-change: finish the change, then run backend/scripts/check.sh step."
+  fi
+  finish fast
+}
+
+tier_step() {
+  local out
+  format_check .
+  if ! out=$(go build ./... 2>&1); then
+    add_section "go build ./..." "$out"
+  else
+    out=$(go vet ./... 2>&1) || add_section "go vet ./..." "$out"
+    arch_check
+    run_tests
+  fi
+  finish step
+}
+
+tier_full() {
+  local out
+  format_check .
+  if ! out=$(go build ./... 2>&1); then
+    add_section "go build ./..." "$out"
+  else
+    out=$(go vet ./... 2>&1) || add_section "go vet ./..." "$out"
+    out=$(go vet -tags perf ./internal/perf/... 2>&1) || add_section "go vet -tags perf ./internal/perf/..." "$out"
+    arch_check
+    run_tests -count=1
+  fi
+  finish full
+}
+
+case "${1:-}" in
+  fast) shift; tier_fast ${1+"$@"} ;;
+  step) tier_step ;;
+  full) tier_full ;;
+  *) echo "usage: check.sh fast <file>... | step | full" >&2; exit 2 ;;
+esac
diff --git a/backend/scripts/gotest-summary.py b/backend/scripts/gotest-summary.py
new file mode 100755
index 00000000..b5bfb6b3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/backend/scripts/gotest-summary.py
@@ -0,0 +1,87 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env python3
+"""Summarise `go test -json ./...` read from stdin.
+
+Line 1 is always the counts. A line starting "NOTE: " warns about skipped tests. If anything failed,
+the failures follow (leaf tests first, then packages that failed to build or crashed) and the exit
+status is 1.
+"""
+import collections
+import json
+import sys
+
+MAX_FAILURES = 5
+MAX_LINES_PER_FAILURE = 10
+NOISE = ("=== RUN", "=== PAUSE", "=== CONT", "=== NAME", "--- PASS", "--- SKIP")
+
+pkg_result = {}
+test_result = {}
+test_output = collections.defaultdict(list)
+pkg_output = collections.defaultdict(list)
+build_output = collections.defaultdict(list)
+failed_build = {}
+skipped = passed = 0
+
+for line in sys.stdin:
+    try:
+        ev = json.loads(line)
+    except ValueError:
+        continue
+    action = ev.get("Action")
+    pkg = ev.get("Package", "")
+    test = ev.get("Test")
+    if action == "build-output":
+        build_output[ev.get("ImportPath", "")].append(ev.get("Output", ""))
+    elif action == "output":
+        (test_output[(pkg, test)] if test else pkg_output[pkg]).append(ev.get("Output", ""))
+    elif action in ("pass", "fail", "skip"):
+        if test:
+            test_result[(pkg, test)] = action
+            if action == "skip":
+                skipped += 1
+            elif action == "pass":
+                passed += 1
+        else:
+            pkg_result[pkg] = action
+            if ev.get("FailedBuild"):
+                failed_build[pkg] = ev["FailedBuild"]
+
+
+def clean(lines):
+    return [l.rstrip("\n") for l in lines if l.strip() and not l.lstrip().startswith(NOISE)]
+
+
+failed_tests = sorted(k for k, v in test_result.items() if v == "fail")
+# A parent test fails whenever one of its subtests does; report only the leaves.
+leaf_failures = [
+    (p, t) for (p, t) in failed_tests
+    if not any(p2 == p and t2.startswith(t + "/") for (p2, t2) in failed_tests)
+]
+failed_pkgs = sorted(p for p, r in pkg_result.items() if r == "fail")
+pkgs_with_failed_test = {p for p, _ in leaf_failures}
+other_failed_pkgs = [p for p in failed_pkgs if p not in pkgs_with_failed_test]
+ok_pkgs = sum(1 for r in pkg_result.values() if r == "pass")
+no_test_pkgs = sum(1 for p, r in pkg_result.items() if r == "skip")
+
+counts = f"{ok_pkgs} packages ok, {len(failed_pkgs)} failed, {no_test_pkgs} without tests; {passed} tests passed, {len(leaf_failures)} failed, {skipped} skipped"
+print(counts)
+if skipped:
+    print(f"NOTE: {skipped} tests skipped. DB-backed tests skip without a local database (DATABASE_URL is cleared), so green here does not cover them.")
+
+shown = 0
+for pkg, test in leaf_failures:
+    if shown == MAX_FAILURES:
+        print(f"... and {len(leaf_failures) - MAX_FAILURES} more failing tests")
+        break
+    shown += 1
+    print(f"FAIL {pkg} {test}")
+    for l in clean(test_output[(pkg, test)])[:MAX_LINES_PER_FAILURE]:
+        print("    " + l)
+
+for pkg in other_failed_pkgs:
+    print(f"FAIL {pkg}")
+    imp = failed_build.get(pkg)
+    lines = clean(build_output[imp]) if imp else clean(pkg_output[pkg])
+    for l in lines[:MAX_LINES_PER_FAILURE]:
+        print("    " + l)
+
+sys.exit(1 if failed_pkgs or leaf_failures else 0)
